### How do you implement with BlockingQueue?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

public class BlockingQueueDemo {
    private final BlockingQueue<String> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100);

    // Producer
    public void produce(String trade) throws InterruptedException {
        queue.put(trade); // blocks if queue is full
    }

    // Consumer
    public String consume() throws InterruptedException {
        return queue.take(); // blocks if queue is empty
    }

    // ZERO synchronization code needed!
    // LinkedBlockingQueue uses two internal locks (putLock + takeLock)
    // allowing concurrent put and take operations.
}
```

### How do you implement with Lock + Conditions?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class ConditionQueueDemo {
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();
    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();
    private final Queue<String> buffer = new LinkedList<>();
    private final int capacity;

    public ConditionQueueDemo(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }

    public void produce(String trade) throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (buffer.size() >= capacity) notFull.await(); // while, not if!
            buffer.add(trade);
            notEmpty.signal(); // wake ONE consumer (targeted)
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }

    public String consume() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (buffer.isEmpty()) notEmpty.await();
            String trade = buffer.poll();
            notFull.signal(); // wake ONE producer (targeted)
            return trade;
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }
}
```

### How do you implement with wait/notifyAll?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class WaitNotifyQueueDemo {
    private final Queue<String> buffer = new LinkedList<>();
    private final int capacity;

    public WaitNotifyQueueDemo(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }

    public synchronized void produce(String trade) throws InterruptedException {
        while (buffer.size() >= capacity) wait();
        buffer.add(trade);
        notifyAll(); // wakes ALL (producers AND consumers) -- wasteful!
    }

    public synchronized String consume() throws InterruptedException {
        while (buffer.isEmpty()) wait();
        String trade = buffer.poll();
        notifyAll(); // wakes ALL -- wasteful!
        return trade;
    }
}
```

### How do the three approaches compare?

| Feature | BlockingQueue | Lock + Conditions | wait/notifyAll |
|---|---|---|---|
| Complexity | Simplest | Medium | Medium |
| Targeted wake-ups | Yes (internal) | Yes (signal) | No (notifyAll) |
| Custom sync code | None | Yes | Yes |
| Production use | Best | Interview exercise | Legacy |
| Two locks | Yes (put + take) | No (one lock) | No (one lock) |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Back-pressure**: a bounded BlockingQueue provides natural back-pressure. When consumers fall behind, producers block. This prevents unbounded memory growth.
- **Disruptor pattern**: for ultra-high-throughput (millions of events per second), the LMAX Disruptor uses a ring buffer with CAS-based sequencing instead of blocking queues.
- **SynchronousQueue**: a BlockingQueue with zero capacity. Each put() blocks until a take() is ready, and vice versa. Used by `newCachedThreadPool()` for direct handoff.
